{"subjectId":"sql","id":"ranking-functions","title":"ROW_NUMBER, RANK, DENSE_RANK & NTILE","group":"Subqueries, CTEs & Windows","order":37,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Ranking functions number rows in a defined order. They differ only in how they treat <strong>ties</strong> — the favourite interview detail.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name, salary,\n       ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS row_num,\n       RANK()       OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk,\n       DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS dense_rnk\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id IN (1, 2)\nORDER BY salary DESC, name;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | salary | row_num | rnk | dense_rnk\n------+--------+---------+-----+----------\nAsha  | 150000 | 1       | 1   | 1\nRavi  | 120000 | 2       | 2   | 2\nPriya | 110000 | 3       | 3   | 3\nKaran | 95000  | 5       | 4   | 4\nMeera | 95000  | 4       | 4   | 4\nNeha  | 72000  | 6       | 6   | 5\nArjun | 70000  | 7       | 7   | 6</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Ties</th><th>Sequence for 150, 120, 110, 95, 95, 72</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ROW_NUMBER()</code></td><td>Arbitrary but unique numbers</td><td>1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6</td></tr><tr><td><code>RANK()</code></td><td>Same rank, then a <strong>gap</strong></td><td>1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 6</td></tr><tr><td><code>DENSE_RANK()</code></td><td>Same rank, <strong>no gap</strong></td><td>1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 5</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>ROW_NUMBER</code> among ties is non-deterministic — Meera and Karan could swap between runs. Add a tie-breaker: <code>ORDER BY salary DESC, emp_id</code>.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-layout-grid\"></i>Ranking within groups</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT dept_id, name, salary,\n       DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rank_in_dept\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id IS NOT NULL\nORDER BY dept_id, rank_in_dept, name;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>dept_id | name   | salary | rank_in_dept\n--------+--------+--------+-------------\n1       | Asha   | 150000 | 1\n1       | Ravi   | 120000 | 2\n1       | Karan  | 95000  | 3\n1       | Meera  | 95000  | 3\n2       | Priya  | 110000 | 1\n2       | Neha   | 72000  | 2\n2       | Arjun  | 70000  | 3\n3       | Sara   | 85000  | 1\n3       | Vikram | 80000  | 2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-numbers\"></i>Top-N per group</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- the 2 best-paid employees of each department\nWITH ranked AS (\n    SELECT dept_id, name, salary,\n           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary DESC, emp_id) AS rn\n    FROM employees\n    WHERE dept_id IS NOT NULL\n)\nSELECT dept_id, name, salary\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn &lt;= 2\nORDER BY dept_id, rn;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>dept_id | name   | salary\n--------+--------+-------\n1       | Asha   | 150000\n1       | Ravi   | 120000\n2       | Priya  | 110000\n2       | Neha   | 72000\n3       | Sara   | 85000\n3       | Vikram | 80000</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Choose the function by the business rule: <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> for <em>exactly N rows</em>, <code>DENSE_RANK</code> for <em>the top N salary levels, including ties</em>.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-pie\"></i>NTILE, PERCENT_RANK, CUME_DIST</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name, salary,\n       NTILE(4)       OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC)          AS quartile,\n       ROUND(PERCENT_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary), 2)     AS pct_rank,\n       ROUND(CUME_DIST()    OVER (ORDER BY salary), 2)     AS cume_dist\nFROM employees\nORDER BY salary DESC, emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | salary | quartile | pct_rank | cume_dist\n-------+--------+----------+----------+----------\nAsha   | 150000 | 1        | 1.0      | 1.0\nRavi   | 120000 | 1        | 0.89     | 0.9\nPriya  | 110000 | 1        | 0.78     | 0.8\nMeera  | 95000  | 2        | 0.56     | 0.7\nKaran  | 95000  | 2        | 0.56     | 0.7\nSara   | 85000  | 2        | 0.44     | 0.5\nVikram | 80000  | 3        | 0.33     | 0.4\nNeha   | 72000  | 3        | 0.22     | 0.3\nArjun  | 70000  | 4        | 0.11     | 0.2\nDev    | 60000  | 4        | 0.0      | 0.1</code></pre></figure>\n<ul><li><code>NTILE(n)</code> splits the ordered rows into <code>n</code> buckets as evenly as possible (earlier buckets get the extra rows: 10 rows → 3, 3, 2, 2).</li><li><code>PERCENT_RANK()</code> = <code>(rank − 1) / (rows − 1)</code> — 0 for the lowest, 1 for the highest.</li><li><code>CUME_DIST()</code> = fraction of rows with a value ≤ the current one.</li></ul>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy-off\"></i>Deduplicating with ROW_NUMBER</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- each customer's most recent order\nSELECT customer_id, order_id, order_date\nFROM (\n    SELECT o.*, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_date DESC) AS rn\n    FROM orders o\n)\nWHERE rn = 1\nORDER BY customer_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>customer_id | order_id | order_date\n------------+----------+-----------\n1           | 108      | 2024-03-15\n2           | 106      | 2024-03-01\n3           | 107      | 2024-03-08\n4           | 105      | 2024-02-20</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>RANK vs DENSE_RANK vs ROW_NUMBER?</summary><div><p>All number rows in window order. <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> always gives unique consecutive numbers (ties broken arbitrarily). <code>RANK</code> gives ties the same number and then skips (1, 2, 2, 4). <code>DENSE_RANK</code> gives ties the same number without skipping (1, 2, 2, 3). For “Nth highest salary” use <code>DENSE_RANK</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}